  ],
  "text": "The sunlight on your face left the Sun eight minutes ago. Light is fast, but it is not instant, and that changes what seeing actually means. Nothing you have ever seen was happening at the moment you saw it. The Moon is a second away. The Sun is eight minutes. Jupiter's light is forty minutes old by the time it reaches your eye. Go further and the delay stops being a technicality. The nearest star you can see tonight sent that light four years ago. Some of the stars up there have already died. The news has not arrived yet. And a faint smudge of a galaxy is showing you light that left before there were humans. There is no now at a distance. Even the person across the room is nanoseconds late. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
